Project Cowboy Plan Document
Python Programming II — Daffa Rabin and Pedro Assuncao
Our project answers how home values and home-value-to-income ratios evolved across
Wyoming counties from 2014 to 2023, with emphasis on counties affected by amenity-driven
gentrification.
The working database comprises a balanced panel of 230 county-year observations across
all 23 Wyoming counties over a 10-year period (2014–2023). Zillow’s County ZHVI supplies
monthly home values, which are be aggregated to annual county averages to integrate with
socioeconomic data. The U.S. Census Bureau’s ACS 5-year estimates provide county-level median
household income, median home values, median rents, seasonal vacancy counts, total housing
units, and population. FHFA’s annual county all-transactions House Price Index (HPI) and the
USDA ERS Natural Amenities Scale serve as price-trend and environmental-attractiveness
benchmarks.
With the database assembled, the analytical workflow consists of three steps:
First, exploratory and spatial data analysis will differentiate state-wide baseline housing
appreciation driven by macro demand and supply constraints also known as decoupling from
amenity-driven gentrification created by external wealth inflows and recreational appeal.
Teton County (Jackson Hole) represents the most popular example, home to national parks,
ski resorts, and high-net-worth real estate acquisition. Famous examples of property owners in
Teton County include Bill Gates, Dick Cheney, Harrison Ford, and Kanye West.
High-amenity counties will be categorized using the top tier of the USDA Natural
Amenities Scale and seasonal vacancy rates. Using geopandas and libpysal, we will compute
Global and Local Moran’s I (LISA cluster maps)1
to evaluate spatial spillover effects, testing
whether real estate inflation in Teton County significantly spills over into adjacent rural counties
(e.g., Lincoln, Sublette, and Fremont).
Second, descriptive analysis will track housing affordability decoupling over time. The
primary dependent variable is the price-to-income ratio, reflecting the growing wedge between
speculative real estate values and local productive earnings. We will compute year-over-year
growth trajectories for home values versus local incomes across counties. Seasonal vacancy rates
(seasonal vacant units divided by total housing units) will serve as our primary proxy for secondary
home and short-term recreational demand.
Third, a two-way fixed effects (TWFE) regression model2 will estimate the impact of
amenity-driven demand and the post-COVID 2020 liquidity shock on cost decoupling:
Decouplingit = β1(Seasonal Vacancyit) + β2(High Amenityi × Post-2020t) + αi + γt + εit
(Decouplingit) will be defined the price-to-income ratio or the housing-income growth gap
for a given county in a given year
(High Amenityi × Post-2020t) captures the post-2020 shock on amenity-rich counties.
(αi ) and (γt) control for time-invariant County Fixed Effects and Yearly Fixed Effects
